EDIT(1)                       Funny CLI Manual                       EDIT(1)

NAME
    edit — Open a file in the terminal editor, because sometimes
           changing one character should require an entire application.

SYNOPSIS
    edit <file>

DESCRIPTION
    The 'edit' command opens the specified file using the CLI's
    built-in terminal editor.

    It resolves the supplied path, creates a TerminalEditor instance,
    and hands control over to it.

    At this point, the command politely gets out of the way and lets
    the editor do its thing.

    In other words:

        edit

    is the command-line equivalent of saying:

        "I have made a terrible typo. Give me the keyboard."

ARGUMENTS
    file
        The file to open in the terminal editor.

        Required. If omitted, the command prints its usage and refuses
        to edit the void.

BEHAVIOR
    The supplied path is converted to an absolute path.

    A TerminalEditor is then created for that path and started with
    'editor.run()'.

    Whatever happens inside the editor is between you and the editor.

    The command does not attempt to interpret your keystrokes, judge
    your formatting, or ask why that variable is named 'x2_final'.

ERRORS
    edit error: <error>
        Something went wrong while launching or running the editor.

        Possible causes include:
        - The file cannot be opened.
        - The path is invalid.
        - The editor encountered a problem.
        - The terminal has decided it would rather watch you suffer.

USAGE ERRORS
    > edit

        Usage: edit <file>

        You brought the editing tool but forgot to bring the file.

EXAMPLES
    > edit README.md
        Opens README.md in the terminal editor.

        Time to fix that typo you've been ignoring for six months.

    > edit src/main.py
        Opens main.py for editing.

        One tiny change.

        Forty-seven minutes later: architecture rewritten.

    > edit todo.txt
        Opens the to-do list.

        Editing the list counts as productivity.

        Probably.

    > edit new-file.txt
        Opens the specified path in the terminal editor.

        Whether the editor creates the file is up to TerminalEditor.

FUN FACTS
    - The command uses a custom 'TerminalEditor' rather than launching
      an external editor.
    - File completion is enabled, because tab completion is faster than
      remembering filenames.
    - The editor receives the resolved absolute path.
    - 'edit' itself does not perform the editing; it delegates the
      important part to the editor.
    - The command catches editor errors and reports them instead of
      silently pretending everything went perfectly.
    - "I'll just make one small change" is one of the most dangerous
      sentences in software development.

